window.SEARCH=[{"t": "OT threat modeling with Microsoft TMT", "p": "OT threat modeling with Microsoft TMT", "u": "index.html", "x": "OT threat modeling with Microsoft TMT # IEC 62443 · MITRE ATT&CK for ICS · Free and open Free templates for the Microsoft Threat Modeling Tool that turn a drawing of your PLCs, SCADA servers, gateways, protocols and security zones into a list of OT-specific threats. Each threat is mapped to IEC 62443 requirements and ATT&CK for ICS techniques and is ready to score. Get started → New to threat modeling? Browse the threats The edge gateway example: a product boundary, its functions inside, and everything it talks to outside."}, {"t": "Two templates", "p": "OT threat modeling with Microsoft TMT", "u": "index.html#two-templates", "x": "OT Product and System For product suppliers and system integrators. What must this PLC, gateway, SCADA package or control system resist, and which requirements does it need? IEC 62443-4-2, 3-3 and 4-1. 110 threats. OT Plant and Site Risk Assessment For asset owners and plant OT security teams. What can go wrong at this site, how bad would it be, and are the existing countermeasures enough? IEC 62443-3-2, 2-1, 2-4 and IEC 61511. 43 threats. Both templates share the same 82 OT stencils, alongside Microsoft's 52 built-in ones, so a diagram drawn with one template opens with the other."}, {"t": "What's inside", "p": "OT threat modeling with Microsoft TMT", "u": "index.html#whats-inside", "x": "82 OT stencils: devices, software, people, data stores, 23 industrial protocols, zones and conduits 110 product and system threats, IDs OT-xxx 43 plant and site threats, IDs PL-xxx 8 threat families, so each threat appears once, where it belongs Part What it is Templates ( .tb7 ) The files you load into TMT. Each holds stencils, properties, threat rules and threat fields. Stencils OT building blocks, grouped by IEC 62443-4-2 component type: embedded (EDR), host (HDR), network (NDR) and software (SAR). See Stencils . Threat library Each threat has a stable ID, STRIDE category, description, mitigations, IEC 62443 references, ATT&CK for ICS techniques and the lowest security level where it applies. See the Threat catalogue . Properties Questions on each element and flow (\"Has a web interface?\", \"Protocol security in use?\"). The answers decide which threats appear. See Properties . Examples An industrial edge gateway and a natural gas pipeline, ready to open. See Examples ."}, {"t": "Where to start", "p": "OT threat modeling with Microsoft TMT", "u": "index.html#where-to-start", "x": "Threat modeling basics Data flow diagrams, trust boundaries, STRIDE, and what makes OT different. Install and quick start From download to your first list of threats in five minutes. Using Microsoft TMT The screens, drawing, generating and reviewing threats. How threats are generated Families, anchors and the Threats on this Flow switch."}, {"t": "Threat modeling basics", "p": "Threat modeling basics", "u": "threat-modeling.html", "x": "Threat modeling basics # Threat modeling is a structured way to find security problems in a design before an attacker does. This page covers what you need before you draw your first OT model."}, {"t": "Four questions", "p": "Threat modeling basics", "u": "threat-modeling.html#four-questions", "x": "Every threat model answers four questions: What are we working on? A diagram of the system and how data moves through it. What can go wrong? The threats against each part of the diagram. What are we going to do about it? Mitigations, requirements, or an accepted risk. Did we do a good job? Review, testing, and keeping the model up to date. The Threat Modeling Tool helps with the first two: you draw the diagram and the template generates the threats. The other two are your review, which you record in TMT against each threat."}, {"t": "The data flow diagram", "p": "Threat modeling basics", "u": "threat-modeling.html#the-data-flow-diagram", "x": "A threat model is drawn as a data flow diagram (DFD) . It shows how data moves, not how cables are laid. Element Shape in TMT OT examples Process : runs code Circle PLC, RTU, safety controller, HMI application, SCADA server, firewall, update service External interactor : outside your control Rectangle Operator, engineer, vendor support, enterprise IT, the physical process, a USB stick Data store : data at rest Two parallel lines Control logic, configuration, firmware image, key store, historian, audit log Data flow : data moving between two elements Arrow Modbus TCP, DNP3, OPC UA, PROFINET, MQTT, a hard-wired trip signal, a firmware download Trust boundary : where trust changes Dashed box or line IEC 62443 zone, conduit, DMZ, device boundary, Internet, physical access boundary Draw the boundaries carefully Attacks happen where data crosses a trust boundary. A flow that enters the control zone from the enterprise network is where most of the important threats appear."}, {"t": "STRIDE", "p": "Threat modeling basics", "u": "threat-modeling.html#stride", "x": "Every threat has one STRIDE category: Threat Violates OT example S Spoofing Authenticity A rogue device sends Modbus writes pretending to be the HMI T Tampering Integrity Controller logic is changed through the engineering protocol R Repudiation Non-repudiation A setpoint change cannot be traced to a person I Information disclosure Confidentiality Project files with network details and passwords are copied D Denial of service Availability A malformed packet stops a protection relay E Elevation of privilege Authorisation A vendor remote session reaches the safety system"}, {"t": "What makes OT different", "p": "Threat modeling basics", "u": "threat-modeling.html#what-makes-ot-different", "x": "Safety and the physical process come first. The worst outcome is not a data breach but an explosion, a spill or a blackout. Each threat states its OT consequence , and the plant template scores consequence severity. Zones and conduits. IEC 62443 groups assets into zones with a target security level ( SL-T , 1 to 4) and connects them through conduits . You draw zones as boundaries. Long lifetimes and legacy. Controllers run for 20 years, often unpatched, on protocols with no authentication. Properties such as Legacy or Unsupported Platform and Protocol Security in Use = None bring these threats in. Two kinds of assessment. A supplier asks whether a product is secure by design (IEC 62443-4-2 components, 3-3 systems). An asset owner asks whether a running site is secure enough (IEC 62443-3-2). That is why there are two templates."}, {"t": "Which template to use", "p": "Threat modeling basics", "u": "threat-modeling.html#which-template-to-use", "x": "You are… Use The threats are about Designing or certifying a product (IEC 62443-4-2) or a system (IEC 62443-3-3) OT Product and System Design: authentication, firmware integrity, secure boot, protocol robustness, the build pipeline Assessing an operating plant or site (IEC 62443-3-2) OT Plant and Site Risk Assessment Operations: patching, remote and vendor access, segmentation, backups, monitoring, people, removable media and the process consequences Doing both Both Model the product first. Its capability security level ( SL-C ) then becomes an input to the plant assessment, where it is compared with the zone's SL-T."}, {"t": "Security levels in one minute", "p": "Threat modeling basics", "u": "threat-modeling.html#security-levels-in-one-minute", "x": "Term Meaning Where it appears SL-T (target) The level a zone or component must reach, from the risk assessment Zone property, component property (record only) SL-C (capability) The level a product can reach when configured correctly, from the supplier Plant property SL-C Meets Zone SL-T SL-A (achieved) The level a zone actually reaches today Zone property in the plant template Relevant from SL The lowest SL at which a threat's countermeasures are normally required Threat field SL 1 protects against casual or accidental violation. SL 2 protects against intentional attacks with simple means. SL 3 protects against sophisticated means with IACS-specific skills. SL 4 protects against the same, with extended resources."}, {"t": "Install and quick start", "p": "Install and quick start", "u": "getting-started.html", "x": "Install and quick start # From download to your first list of OT threats in about five minutes."}, {"t": "Requirements", "p": "Install and quick start", "u": "getting-started.html#requirements", "x": "Windows with the Microsoft Threat Modeling Tool , which is free. The templates are tested with TMT 2016. The two template files from this repository."}, {"t": "Quick start", "p": "Install and quick start", "u": "getting-started.html#quick-start", "x": "Install TMT Download and install the Microsoft Threat Modeling Tool. Get the templates Download this repository (green Code button > Download ZIP , or git clone ). Choose the template Open TMT. Under Template For New Models , click Browse and choose OT Product and System Threat Template (IEC 62443).tb7 or OT Plant and Site Risk Assessment Template (IEC 62443).tb7 . Create a model Click Create A Model . The OT stencils appear in the Stencils pane on the right. Draw and answer Drag in stencils, connect them with protocol flows, draw zones around them, and answer the properties of each element and flow. Generate Choose View > Analysis View . TMT generates the threats for every flow. Review and report Review each threat, then use Reports > Create Full Report . See it working first Open one of the examples with Open A Model and switch to Analysis View . The edge gateway generates 138 threats and the gas pipeline 114."}, {"t": "What is in the download", "p": "Install and quick start", "u": "getting-started.html#what-is-in-the-download", "x": "OT Product and System Threat Template (IEC 62443).tb7 product and system template OT Plant and Site Risk Assessment Template (IEC 62443).tb7 plant and site template examples/ two example models docs/ this website LICENSE MIT licence"}, {"t": "Next steps", "p": "Install and quick start", "u": "getting-started.html#next-steps", "x": "Using Microsoft TMT : the screens, drawing, generating and reviewing. Product and system threat model or Plant and site risk assessment : the full method."}, {"t": "Using Microsoft TMT", "p": "Using Microsoft TMT", "u": "using-tmt.html", "x": "Using Microsoft TMT # How to draw a model, answer its properties, generate the threats and review them in the Microsoft Threat Modeling Tool."}, {"t": "The screens", "p": "Using Microsoft TMT", "u": "using-tmt.html#the-screens", "x": "Screen How to get there What you do there Home Start TMT Choose the template, create or open a model, open a template for editing Design View Default view of a model Draw the diagram Stencils pane Right side of Design View Pick elements: OT stencils grouped by type, plus Microsoft's Element Properties pane Select an element or flow Name it and answer its properties Analysis View View > Analysis View See the generated threats in the Threat List and review each one Messages Bottom tab Warnings about the model, such as flows that are not connected Reports Reports menu Full HTML report of the model and its threats Design View with the edge gateway example open, and the Element Properties pane on the right."}, {"t": "Draw the diagram", "p": "Using Microsoft TMT", "u": "using-tmt.html#draw-the-diagram", "x": "Place elements Drag a stencil from the Stencils pane onto the canvas. Use the most specific stencil, for example EDR PLC or Process Controller rather than Generic Process . The stencil decides which technology threats apply and fixes the properties it already knows. Connect them with flows Drag a protocol stencil (for example Modbus TCP ) onto the canvas, then drag each end of the arrow onto an element until it snaps. A flow goes one way. Draw two flows when both directions matter, for example commands down and status up. Draw trust boundaries Border boundaries (IEC 62443 Security Zone, Safety Zone SIS, Industrial DMZ, Component or Device Boundary, Enterprise or IT Zone, Vendor or Cloud Boundary) are boxes: drag them on and resize them around the elements in the zone. Line boundaries (IEC 62443 Conduit, Internet or Untrusted Network Boundary, Physical Access Boundary, Process or Privilege Boundary) are lines that you draw across the flows they cut. Name everything Names appear in the threat titles (\"Spoofing of commands from Engineering Workstation to Line 3 PLC \"), so make them meaningful. Answer the properties Select each element and flow and answer its questions in Element Properties. See Properties . Save Save the model as a .tm7 file. The template is stored inside the model, so the file opens on another machine without the template. Stay inside the drawing area Keep the drawing within about 1800 × 1100 pixels. TMT reports elements placed much further out as corrupted whe"}, {"t": "Answer the properties", "p": "Using Microsoft TMT", "u": "using-tmt.html#answer-the-properties", "x": "A software stencil: the hardware questions are fixed to Not applicable under Predefined Static Attributes. A flow has seven questions, including Threats on this Flow . Every question starts at Unknown , which keeps its threats in the model. That is safe but noisy, so answer No where something does not exist. Greyed-out values under Predefined Static Attributes are fixed by the stencil. For example, software has no debug ports, and HTTPS always has integrity and encryption. Questions marked (record only) document the model but do not change the threats."}, {"t": "Generate and review threats", "p": "Using Microsoft TMT", "u": "using-tmt.html#generate-and-review-threats", "x": "Switch to Analysis View View > Analysis View . TMT generates threats for every data flow and shows the count in the status bar, for example \"138 Threats Displayed\". Read a threat Select it in the Threat List. Its description, mitigations and references appear in Threat Properties. Set its state Not Started : not reviewed yet. Needs Investigation : an open question. Not Applicable : the threat cannot happen here; write why in Justification . Mitigated : controls are in place; name them in the mitigation fields. Score it Fill in the risk fields. See Reviewing threats and scoring risk . Sort and filter the Threat List with its column headers, for example by Priority or by Interaction (the flow). When you change the diagram, TMT keeps your review of threats that still apply and adds new ones."}, {"t": "Other useful functions", "p": "Using Microsoft TMT", "u": "using-tmt.html#other-useful-functions", "x": "Function Where Use it to Apply Template File > Apply Template Move an existing model to a newer template version, or to the other template Threat Model Information File > Threat Model Information Record the scope, owner, assumptions and external dependencies Out Of Scope Checkbox on any element Exclude an element with TMT's own switch. The OT templates also offer Assessment Scope , where Supplier evidence keeps the threats but lets you cite the supplier's certificate Open Template Home screen Look inside a template: stencils, threat rules, properties Create Full Report Reports HTML report with the diagram, the threats per flow and your review Close a model before opening another TMT 2016 can crash when you open a second model while another model's threat list is still shown. Use File > Close Model first."}, {"t": "Product and system threat model", "p": "Product and system threat model", "u": "product-guide.html", "x": "Product and system threat model # The method for an IEC 62443-4-2 component (a PLC, gateway or HMI software) or an IEC 62443-3-3 system (a control system solution), using the OT Product and System template. 1 Scope → 2 Draw → 3 Properties → 4 Anchors → 5 Generate → 6 Review → 7 Requirements → 8 Report and maintain"}, {"t": "1. Scope and assumptions", "p": "Product and system threat model", "u": "product-guide.html#1-scope-and-assumptions", "x": "Write down what is in scope (the product, its interfaces, its development and update process), its intended use, and your assumptions, for example \"installed in a locked cabinet in a zone with SL-T 2\". Record them under File > Threat Model Information ."}, {"t": "2. Draw the diagram", "p": "Product and system threat model", "u": "product-guide.html#2-draw-the-diagram", "x": "Component model (IEC 62443-4-2) # Draw a Component or Device Boundary around the product. Inside it, split the product into its functions: the runtime, web interface, update service, key store and firmware image. Separate functions with a Process or Privilege Boundary where isolation matters, such as containers, kernel and user space, or a safety partition. Outside it, add everything the product talks to: engineers, operators, the PLCs it polls, the cloud, a USB stick. System model (IEC 62443-3-3) # Draw one IEC 62443 Security Zone per zone. Add a Safety Zone SIS , an Industrial DMZ Level 3.5 and an Enterprise or IT Zone as needed. Set the Purdue level and zone SL-T on each. Connect zones with the flows that really exist. Use IEC 62443 Conduit and Internet or Untrusted Network Boundary lines for the paths between them. Pick the stencil that matches each device. Its IEC 62443-4-2 component type (EDR, HDR, NDR or SAR) is fixed by the stencil. Always # Add a Product Supplier and Development Environment with one Software Supply and Build Pipeline flow to the product. It carries the build and code-signing threats exactly once."}, {"t": "3. Answer the properties", "p": "Product and system threat model", "u": "product-guide.html#3-answer-the-properties", "x": "On Answer Each component Web interface, user accounts, stored secrets, field updates, third-party code, debug ports, physical exposure, wireless, USB, legacy platform Each flow Protocol security, control commands, safety data, secrets, physical medium Each data store Sensitive data, control-critical data Answer No where something does not exist, which removes threats that cannot apply. Software stencils already fix the hardware questions to Not applicable . Every question and the threats it controls are listed in Properties ."}, {"t": "4. One anchor flow per component", "p": "Product and system threat model", "u": "product-guide.html#4-one-anchor-flow-per-component", "x": "A component's own threats, such as default passwords, unsigned firmware or missing logging, should appear once and not on every flow into it. Set Threats on this Flow = All on one flow into each component, normally the most exposed one. Set Without component threats on the other flows into it. If you leave everything at Auto , you get the older 1.x behaviour, which has some repeats. See How threats are generated ."}, {"t": "5. Generate threats", "p": "Product and system threat model", "u": "product-guide.html#5-generate-threats", "x": "Switch to View > Analysis View ."}, {"t": "6. Review and score", "p": "Product and system threat model", "u": "product-guide.html#6-review-and-score", "x": "Go through every threat. Decide whether it applies and what already mitigates it, then score its likelihood and impact. Sort by Relevant from SL to see what your target SL requires. See Reviewing threats and scoring risk ."}, {"t": "7. Requirements and mitigations", "p": "Product and system threat model", "u": "product-guide.html#7-requirements-and-mitigations", "x": "Each threat names the IEC 62443-4-2 component requirements (CR, EDR, HDR, NDR, SAR) or 3-3 system requirements (SR) that address it. Use them as product requirements, test cases and certification evidence. Stable IDs Threat IDs such as OT-S01 or OT-T23R never change between versions, so you can reference them from requirements, test reports and risk registers."}, {"t": "8. Report and maintain", "p": "Product and system threat model", "u": "product-guide.html#8-report-and-maintain", "x": "Create the full report. Keep the .tm7 with the design. Update it when interfaces, protocols or the update process change, and review it at each release, as IEC 62443-4-1 practice 2 (SR-2, threat model) requires."}, {"t": "Plant and site risk assessment", "p": "Plant and site risk assessment", "u": "plant-guide.html", "x": "Plant and site risk assessment # The method for an asset owner assessing an operating site, following the IEC 62443-3-2 zone and conduit requirements (ZCR), using the OT Plant and Site Risk Assessment template."}, {"t": "What the plant threats cover", "p": "Plant and site risk assessment", "u": "plant-guide.html#what-the-plant-threats-cover", "x": "Zones and conduits: unfiltered flows between zones, unauthenticated control traffic, paths from the business network or the Internet to controllers, unmanaged cellular routers, wireless. Remote and third-party access: stolen remote access credentials, always-on vendor connections, remote takeover of operator sessions, integrators working without security requirements. Assets: unpatched and end-of-life systems, missing malware protection, unhardened configuration, shared and leftover accounts, no monitoring, no tested backup, capability below the zone target (SL-C < SL-T), ransomware spread. Operations and process: attacks that drive the process to a hazardous state, unmanaged logic changes, safety system compromise and SIS independence, loss of view without manual fallback, alarm suppression, permissive firewall rules, IT scanning of fragile devices. People, media and data: USB media and visiting laptops, phishing, insider misuse, untraceable actions, theft of plant documents, backups encrypted together with the plant, unreviewed alerts, physical access at unattended sites, cloud service compromise. References use IEC 62443-2-1:2024 programme elements (for example 2-1 NET 3 secure remote access), IEC 62443-2-4 service provider capabilities ( 2-4 SP.07 ), IEC 62443-3-2 ( 3-2 ZCR 5 ), IEC 62443-3-3 system requirements, and IEC 61511-1 clause 8.2.4."}, {"t": "Step by step", "p": "Plant and site risk assessment", "u": "plant-guide.html#step-by-step", "x": "Step IEC 62443-3-2 What to do in TMT 1. System under consideration ZCR 1 Draw the site's assets with the same stencils as the product template. Include the people and the external connections: vendors, enterprise IT, cloud, laptops, USB media. 2. Initial risk ZCR 2 Note the worst-case consequence of each zone in the zone properties. 3. Zones and conduits ZCR 3 Draw each zone as a border boundary, and connect zones only through flows that really exist. A firewall or data diode between zones is a component (NDR stencil) on the path. 4. Zone properties ZCR 4, 6 Set the Purdue level, zone SL-T, worst-case consequence, safety or environmental functions, physical access and achieved SL (SL-A). These record the inputs; they do not change the threats. 5. Asset and flow properties ZCR 5 For each asset, set its site state: patch status, hardening, malware protection, shared accounts, monitoring, tested backup, SL-C meets zone SL-T, worst-case consequence. For each flow: protocol security, filtered by a firewall or conduit device, permanent connection. These decide which threats appear. 6. Detailed risk assessment ZCR 5 In Analysis View, record for each threat the existing countermeasures, consequence severity, likelihood, current risk, whether it is within tolerable risk, the treatment, planned countermeasures and residual risk. 7. Document and track ZCR 6, 7 Create the full report. Use Action Owner , Due Date and Verification Evidence to track the treatment plan."}, {"t": "Safety instrumented systems", "p": "Plant and site risk assessment", "u": "plant-guide.html#safety-instrumented-systems", "x": "If the site has a SIS, the plant threats also cover the IEC 61511-1 clause 8.2.4 security risk assessment: SIS independence, remote reset of the ESD, and connections to the safety network. Put the SIS in its own Safety Zone SIS . Mark safety controllers and safety I/O as safety-related. Their stencils already do this. Draw hard-wired trip inputs with Physical Medium = Hardwired signal . They then carry the field wiring threat (PL-T11) instead of network threats. Link each threat to the process hazard through the HAZOP or Cyber HAZOP Reference field."}, {"t": "Combining with a product model", "p": "Plant and site risk assessment", "u": "plant-guide.html#combining-with-a-product-model", "x": "If a supplier has modelled the product with the product template, take its capability security level (SL-C) and answer Component Capability (SL-C) Meets Zone SL-T on the asset. A No brings in the SL gap threat, so the gap is recorded and treated. See the gas pipeline example for a complete assessment."}, {"t": "Reviewing threats and scoring risk", "p": "Reviewing threats and scoring risk", "u": "review.html", "x": "Reviewing threats and scoring risk # Each generated threat comes with content you read and fields you fill in. This page explains both, and gives a review routine that works."}, {"t": "What each threat tells you", "p": "Reviewing threats and scoring risk", "u": "review.html#what-each-threat-tells-you", "x": "Field Content Title Names the real source and target, for example \"Spoofing of Engineering Workstation towards Line 3 PLC\" Description What could happen and why Priority High, Medium or Low Category STRIDE Possible Mitigation(s) / Recommended Countermeasure(s) Controls that address it MITRE ATT&CK for ICS Related techniques (v20 IDs) IEC 62443 Requirement(s) 4-2 CR/EDR/HDR/NDR/SAR, 3-3 SR, 2-1, 2-4, 3-2 or IEC 61511 references Foundational Requirement FR1 to FR7 Relevant from SL Lowest SL-T at which countermeasures are normally required OT Consequence Safety, environment, equipment, production, view or control, IP"}, {"t": "Fields you fill in", "p": "Reviewing threats and scoring risk", "u": "review.html#fields-you-fill-in", "x": "Product and System template Plant and Site template Likelihood (1-5) Existing Countermeasures Impact (1-5) HAZOP or Cyber HAZOP Reference Risk Rating Consequence Category Risk Treatment Consequence Severity (1-5) and Likelihood (1-5) Implemented Mitigation(s) Current Risk and Within Tolerable Risk Verification Evidence Risk Treatment, Planned Countermeasure(s) and Residual Risk Owner Action Owner, Due Date, Verification Evidence"}, {"t": "Risk matrix", "p": "Reviewing threats and scoring risk", "u": "review.html#risk-matrix", "x": "Risk = likelihood × impact (or consequence severity), on a 5 × 5 matrix. Score Rating 20-25 Critical 12-19 High 6-11 Medium 1-5 Low Treatment options are mitigate , transfer , avoid or accept . Record accepted risks together with the owner who accepted them."}, {"t": "A review routine that works", "p": "Reviewing threats and scoring risk", "u": "review.html#a-review-routine-that-works", "x": "Go flow by flow Sort the Threat List by Interaction and walk the diagram one flow at a time. Fix properties, not threats If a threat cannot apply because of something you forgot to answer, such as \"this device has no web interface\", correct the property. The threat then disappears for good, and also from the next model built on it. Mark the rest Use Not Applicable with a one-line justification, or Mitigated with the controls named. Score and treat Fill in likelihood, impact and treatment for what remains. Check against your SL-T Sort by Relevant from SL . Everything at or below your target SL should be mitigated or explicitly accepted."}, {"t": "Do I need CVSS?", "p": "Reviewing threats and scoring risk", "u": "review.html#do-i-need-cvss", "x": "No. CVSS scores known vulnerabilities in products. A threat model looks at design-level threats before any vulnerability exists, so it uses likelihood × impact (or consequence severity), as IEC 62443-3-2 does. CVSS becomes useful later, when a specific vulnerability (a CVE) is found in a component you modelled."}, {"t": "How threats are generated", "p": "How threats are generated", "u": "how-threats-work.html", "x": "How threats are generated # TMT generates threats per data flow. Version 2.0 of the templates makes sure each threat appears once, where it belongs."}, {"t": "The problem", "p": "How threats are generated", "u": "how-threats-work.html#the-problem", "x": "For every flow, TMT checks every threat rule against the flow's source, its target and the flow itself. Without care, a threat about a component, such as default credentials on a PLC, would appear once for every flow into that PLC. In one real review made with version 1.3, about half of the generated threats were judged repeats or not applicable."}, {"t": "Families and anchors", "p": "How threats are generated", "u": "how-threats-work.html#families-and-anchors", "x": "Every threat belongs to one family , and each family has one anchor : the only kind of flow on which TMT generates it. Family What it is about Generated on 1 Channel Sniffing, spoofing, replay, injection on the wire Flows that cross a zone or network boundary over a network medium 2 Technology Weaknesses of the target's stencil type (web UI, HMI, OPC server, safety controller) The component's anchor flow 3 Component Lifecycle of the target: credentials, patching, firmware, debug ports, crypto, logging The component's anchor flow 4 Host Malware, OS hardening, attacker tools on a host The host's anchor flow; not on software marked Runs on a Host Shown in the Model 5 Path One attacker path: this source reaching this target (vendor access, insider, USB, cloud commands) Every flow where the path exists 6 Product lifecycle Build pipeline and code-signing compromise The Software Supply and Build Pipeline flow, once per product 7 Local boundary Isolation bypass, wiring tampering Flows crossing a Process or Privilege or Physical Access boundary, and hard-wired signals 8 Data at rest Tampering, disclosure or loss of stored data The store's anchor flow in, or one flow out of a store nobody writes to (IDs ending in R)"}, {"t": "The switch: Threats on this Flow", "p": "How threats are generated", "u": "how-threats-work.html#the-switch-threats-on-this-flow", "x": "This flow property chooses which families a flow shows: Value Channel and local boundary (1, 7) Path (5) Component, host, data at rest (2, 3, 4, 8) Use it for Auto (default) ✓ ✓ Only if the flow crosses a trust boundary or comes from an external interactor Drawing by hand; the 1.x behaviour All ✓ ✓ ✓ The anchor: one flow into each component Without component threats ✓ ✓ – The other flows into that component Component threats only – – ✓ An anchor flow whose channel is already shown elsewhere Path threats only – ✓ – A parallel flow over a channel already shown None – – – A flow drawn only for context Which flow should be the anchor? For each component, set All on the flow into it that is most exposed: the one that crosses a trust boundary or comes from outside, carries control commands, and runs over a network. Set Without component threats on its other incoming flows. In Analysis View, sort the Threat List by Title : a component threat that appears twice for the same component means a second flow still has All or Auto ."}, {"t": "Other rules", "p": "How threats are generated", "u": "how-threats-work.html#other-rules", "x": "Non-network media. A flow with Physical Medium = Hardwired signal (a 4-20 mA loop, a trip input) or Local operator input is not a network channel. The sniffing and spoofing threats are skipped, and the wiring tampering threat (OT-T30, PL-T11) applies instead. Specific beats generic. Where a specific threat covers an attack, the generic one steps aside. Wireless sniffing (OT-I10) replaces generic sniffing (OT-I01) on a wireless link. Safety protocol tampering (OT-T11) replaces generic tampering on safety flows. Network-device DoS (OT-D08) replaces generic DoS on switches and firewalls. Properties that cannot apply are fixed. Software stencils fix the hardware questions to Not applicable , so they never generate hardware threats. Scope. Assessment Scope = Out of scope on an element removes its threats. Threats on this Flow = None removes a flow's threats. Data that is only read. For a store nobody writes to, such as a TPM key store, set Show Data Store Threats for Source Store = Yes on one flow out of it. Its data-at-rest threats then appear with IDs ending in R."}, {"t": "What this gives you", "p": "How threats are generated", "u": "how-threats-work.html#what-this-gives-you", "x": "Example 1.3 2.0 Industrial edge gateway (product) 230 threats 138 threats Natural gas pipeline (plant) 108 threats 114 threats: every asset now gets its site threats once, including assets inside a zone that 1.3 skipped"}, {"t": "Properties", "p": "Properties", "u": "properties.html", "x": "Properties # Properties are the questions in TMT's Element Properties pane. The answers decide which threats appear."}, {"t": "How properties work", "p": "Properties", "u": "properties.html#how-properties-work", "x": "Everything starts at Unknown , which keeps the threat. Answer No to remove threats that cannot apply. Properties that cannot apply to a stencil, or that the stencil already answers, are fixed and shown greyed out under Predefined Static Attributes . Software has no debug ports, USB, radio or physical exposure. A safety controller is always safety-related. A wireless link always has the Wireless medium. HTTPS and IPsec always have integrity and encryption. Properties marked (record only) , and the zone properties, document the model but do not change the threats. TMT rules can only see the two ends of a flow and the flow itself, not the zone around them. Microsoft's built-in stencils (OS Process, SQL Database, HTTPS and so on) can be drawn too, with the OT properties only."}, {"t": "At a glance", "p": "Properties", "u": "properties.html#at-a-glance", "x": "Element Product and System template Plant template adds Component Component type · SL-T (record only) · Safety-related · Reachable from outside its zone · Web interface · User accounts · Stores credentials or keys · Field-updatable · Third-party components · Debug ports · Physically exposed · Wireless · Removable media · Legacy platform · Runs on a host shown in the model · Assessment scope Patch status · Hardened · Malware protection · Shared accounts · Monitored · Backup tested · SL-C meets zone SL-T · Worst-case consequence Data flow Protocol security · Carries control commands · Carries safety data · Carries secrets · Physical medium · Threats on this flow · Show data store threats for source store Filtered by firewall or conduit device · Permanent connection Data store Sensitive data · Control-critical data · Assessment scope Offline or immutable copy External interactor Trust level (record only) · Assessment scope Third party or remote access · Security training Zone Purdue level · Zone SL-T Worst-case consequence · Safety or environmental functions · Physical access · SL-A"}, {"t": "Product and System template", "p": "Properties", "u": "properties.html#product-and-system-template", "x": "The last column lists, for each value, the threats it adds (they are generated only with that value) or removes . Click an ID to open it in the threat catalogue. Components and assets (processes) Property (as shown in TMT) What it means and how to set it Values Threats affected IEC 62443-4-2 Component Type IEC 62443-4-2 component class. Fixed by the stencil you pick (EDR embedded device, HDR host device, NDR network device, SAR software application) and decides which device-class threats apply. Only set it by hand on the Generic Process. Not Selected, EDR - Embedded device, HDR - Host device, NDR - Network device, SAR - Software application Through the stencil: the device-class threats Security Level Target (SL-T IEC 62443) (record only) Security level the component must meet (SL-T). Record only: take it from the zone or your product requirements; it does not change the generated threats. Not Selected, SL 1, SL 2, SL 3, SL 4 None (record only) Performs Safety-Related Function (SIS or SIL) Yes if the component performs or protects a safety function (SIS logic solver, safety I/O, a device that can cause a hazard). Adds the safety threats. Unknown, Yes, No Yes : adds OT-T10 , OT-E04 , OT-E05 ; removes OT-T04 Reachable from Outside its Zone (remote or vendor access) Yes if the component can be reached from outside its own zone, for example by VPN, remote desktop or a vendor connection. Unknown, Yes, No Yes : adds OT-S06 Has Web or HTTP(S) Management Interface Yes if the component"}, {"t": "Plant and Site template", "p": "Properties", "u": "properties.html#plant-and-site-template", "x": "The plant template keeps all the properties above and adds these questions about how the site is run. Components and assets (processes) Property (as shown in TMT) What it means and how to set it Values Threats affected Patch Status Up to date: current vendor-approved patches installed. Patches pending: known patches not yet installed. End of life or unpatchable: no more vendor fixes. Unknown counts as not patched. Unknown, Up to date, Patches pending, End of life or unpatchable Unknown : adds PL-E06 Patches pending : adds PL-E06 End of life or unpatchable : adds PL-E07 Hardened to Site Security Baseline Yes if the asset is configured to the site security baseline (unused services, ports and accounts removed, vendor hardening guide applied). Unknown, Yes, No Unknown : adds PL-E08 No : adds PL-E08 Malware Protection or Application Allow-listing Yes if anti-malware or application allow-listing is installed and maintained. Not applicable for devices that cannot run it (PLCs, drives). Unknown, Yes, No, Not applicable Unknown : adds PL-T04 No : adds PL-T04 Shared or Generic Accounts in Use Yes if people log in with default, shared or generic accounts (for example 'operator', 'admin'). Unknown, Yes, No Unknown : adds PL-S03 , PL-R02 Yes : adds PL-S03 , PL-R02 Logs or Traffic Monitored by Security Team Yes if the asset's security events are collected and someone reviews them, or its network traffic is monitored by an OT IDS. Unknown, Yes, No Unknown : adds PL-R01 No : adds PL-R01 Bac"}, {"t": "Threat catalogue", "p": "Threat catalogue", "u": "threats.html", "x": "Threat catalogue # All threats of both templates. Filter by template, STRIDE category, family or priority, or search any text, including ATT&CK and IEC 62443 references. Click a row to see the description, mitigation and references. In TMT, the titles name the actual source and target elements of the flow instead of \"source\" and \"target\". window.CAT_LABELS={\"product\": \"Product and System\", \"plant\": \"Plant and Site\", \"search\": \"Search ID, title, mitigation, ATT&CK, IEC…\", \"allStride\": \"All STRIDE\", \"allFam\": \"All families\", \"allPri\": \"All priorities\", \"shown\": \"{n} of {t} threats · click a row for details\", \"cols\": {\"stride\": \"STRIDE\", \"threat\": \"Threat\", \"fam\": \"Family\", \"pri\": \"Priority\", \"desc\": \"Description\", \"mit\": \"Mitigation\", \"fr\": \"Foundational requirement\", \"impact\": \"OT consequence\"}, \"stride\": {\"S\": \"Spoofing\", \"T\": \"Tampering\", \"R\": \"Repudiation\", \"I\": \"Information disclosure\", \"D\": \"Denial of service\", \"E\": \"Elevation of privilege\"}, \"fam\": {\"1\": \"1 Channel\", \"2\": \"2 Technology\", \"3\": \"3 Component\", \"4\": \"4 Host\", \"5\": \"5 Path\", \"6\": \"6 Product lifecycle\", \"7\": \"7 Local boundary\", \"8\": \"8 Data at rest\"}};"}, {"t": "IDs", "p": "Threat catalogue", "u": "threats.html#ids", "x": "OT- product and system threats, PL- plant and site threats. The letter after the dash is the STRIDE category. IDs ending in R are the data-at-rest threats for a store that is only read from. See How threats are generated . IDs are stable across versions, so you can reference them from requirements, tests and risk registers."}, {"t": "Stencils", "p": "Stencils", "u": "stencils.html", "x": "Stencils # 82 OT stencils, shared by both templates, plus Microsoft's 52 built-in stencils. Component stencils are prefixed with their IEC 62443-4-2 type: EDR embedded device, HDR host device, NDR network device, SAR software application. Icon colour shows the type: blue embedded device, teal host device, orange network device, green software application, slate interactor, purple data store, brown protocol, red boundary. Embedded devices (EDR) · 7 Stencil (as shown in TMT) What it represents EDR PLC or Process Controller Programmable logic controller or DCS controller executing control logic (IEC 61131-3). Typical interfaces: engineering protocol, fieldbus, I/O, web UI. EDR Safety Controller SIS SIL rated Safety instrumented system logic solver (IEC 61508 / IEC 61511). Treat any compromise as a potential loss-of-safety event. EDR RTU or Remote Field Controller Remote terminal unit or remote controller, often at unattended sites, using DNP3, IEC 60870-5-104 or Modbus over WAN, radio or cellular. EDR IED or Protection Relay Intelligent electronic device or protection relay (IEC 61850, DNP3). Protection functions are safety- and grid-critical. EDR Smart Field Device sensor actuator drive analyzer Level 0/1 device such as a transmitter, positioner, VFD, servo drive or analyzer with digital communication (HART, PROFINET, IO-Link, EtherNet/IP). EDR IIoT Device or Edge Sensor Connected sensor or device that sends telemetry to an edge gateway or cloud (MQTT, OPC UA, HTTPS). EDR Safet"}, {"t": "Examples", "p": "Examples", "u": "examples.html", "x": "Examples # Two complete models are in the examples folder. Open them with Open A Model and switch to Analysis View ."}, {"t": "Industrial edge gateway (product template)", "p": "Examples", "u": "examples.html#industrial-edge-gateway-product-template", "x": "examples/Industrial Edge Gateway - Product Threat Model.tm7 models an industrial IoT edge gateway as an IEC 62443-4-2 component. It generates 138 threats . Where Elements Inside the product boundary Southbound Protocol Service, Container Runtime and Apps (behind a Container isolation privilege boundary), Cloud Connector, Web Management UI, Gateway Firmware and OS, Firmware Update Agent, Gateway Configuration, Local Audit Log, Key Store in TPM, Firmware Image A and B Vendor and cloud, beyond an Internet boundary Cloud IoT Platform, Vendor Update Server Plant side Machine PLCs and Drives, Maintenance Engineer, Vendor Service Technician, USB Stick for Configuration Import, Site Time Server, Gateway Vendor Development and Build What it shows: Machines are polled over Modbus TCP without security . Telemetry and commands use MQTT over TLS . Firmware is downloaded over HTTPS, verified and written to an A/B image. Keys are in a TPM. The hardware platform is physically exposed, with debug ports, USB and wireless. Its device threats (secure boot, debug interfaces, key extraction) appear once, on its anchor flow. The software functions run on it, so host threats are not repeated on them. The supplier's build pipeline flow carries the supply chain threats ( OT-T28 , OT-S12 ). The container boundary carries the isolation threat ( OT-E23 ). The read-only key store gets its data-at-rest threats on its outgoing flow ( OT-I11R )."}, {"t": "Natural gas pipeline (plant template)", "p": "Examples", "u": "examples.html#natural-gas-pipeline-plant-template", "x": "examples/Gas Pipeline - Site Risk Assessment.tm7 assesses a gas transmission pipeline. It generates 114 threats . Zone (consequence, SL-T / SL-A) Elements Enterprise IT, Level 4 (minor) Corporate IT and Gas Scheduling Control Centre DMZ, Level 3.5 (moderate) Remote Access Gateway, DMZ Historian Pipeline Control Centre, Levels 2 and 3 (major, SL-T 2 / SL-A 2) Pipeline SCADA Server, Pipeline Controller HMI, Engineering Workstation, Leak Detection System (end of life), Pipeline Historian Compressor Station, Levels 1 and 0 (catastrophic, unattended, SL-T 2 / SL-A 1) Station Cellular Router, Station RTU, Compressor Unit Control PLC, Compressor Unit and Station Piping Station Safety Zone, ESD (catastrophic, unattended, SL-T 3 / SL-A 1) Station ESD System Block Valve Site, Level 1 (major, unattended, SL-T 2 / SL-A 0) Valve Site RTU, Mainline Valve Actuator Outside the zones Compressor OEM Remote Support (always-on VPN), Pipeline Controller, Field Technician Laptop What the assessment brings out: Remote sites over public networks: station telemetry runs DNP3 without security over cellular, and the valve site uses licensed radio. Business dependency: gas nominations go from corporate scheduling straight into SCADA without a conduit device. This is the IT-to-OT dependency behind the 2021 Colonial Pipeline shutdown ( PL-E01 , PL-E02 ). Safety: the compressor PLC can reset the ESD remotely, so the safety system is reachable from the control side ( PL-E10 , PL-T07 ). The hard-wired trip i"}, {"t": "Troubleshooting and FAQ", "p": "Troubleshooting and FAQ", "u": "faq.html", "x": "Troubleshooting and FAQ #"}, {"t": "TMT says the model is corrupted", "p": "Troubleshooting and FAQ", "u": "faq.html#tmt-says-the-model-is-corrupted", "x": "Usually the message is \"Line element coordinates are corrupted\" or \"Border … will be corrected\". It means an element or line lies outside TMT's drawing area. Keep the drawing within about 1800 × 1100 pixels."}, {"t": "TMT crashes when I open a second model", "p": "Troubleshooting and FAQ", "u": "faq.html#tmt-crashes-when-i-open-a-second-model", "x": "TMT 2016 can crash (\"Unhandled exception\") when you open a model while another model's threat list is still showing. Close the first model with File > Close Model before opening the next."}, {"t": "The same threat appears on many flows", "p": "Troubleshooting and FAQ", "u": "faq.html#the-same-threat-appears-on-many-flows", "x": "Set Threats on this Flow = All on one flow into the component and Without component threats on the others. For parallel flows over the same channel, set Path threats only on all but one. See How threats are generated ."}, {"t": "A threat cannot apply to my system", "p": "Troubleshooting and FAQ", "u": "faq.html#a-threat-cannot-apply-to-my-system", "x": "First check whether a property removes it. The Properties page lists which value removes which threat. Answering the property makes the threat disappear for good. If no property fits, mark the threat Not Applicable with a justification."}, {"t": "There are no threats on the zones themselves", "p": "Troubleshooting and FAQ", "u": "faq.html#there-are-no-threats-on-the-zones-themselves", "x": "TMT only generates threats for data flows. Zone properties record the risk assessment inputs. Threats that depend on the zone use the properties of the assets and flows inside it."}, {"t": "Can I use Microsoft's own threats too?", "p": "Troubleshooting and FAQ", "u": "faq.html#can-i-use-microsofts-own-threats-too", "x": "These templates contain only OT threats. Microsoft's stencils are included so that the IT parts of a system can be drawn. For Microsoft's web and cloud threats, model those parts with Microsoft's templates."}, {"t": "The templates are in English. Can I use them for Korean projects?", "p": "Troubleshooting and FAQ", "u": "faq.html#the-templates-are-in-english-can-i-use-them-for-korean-projects", "x": "Yes. The stencils, properties and threat texts appear in TMT in English, which keeps the threat IDs and standard references consistent across teams. You can write names, justifications and review fields in Korean, and TMT saves them as entered."}, {"t": "I have models made with version 1.x", "p": "Troubleshooting and FAQ", "u": "faq.html#i-have-models-made-with-version-1x", "x": "Apply the 2.0 template with File > Apply Template , then set the anchor flows. Threat IDs are unchanged, so existing reviews stay valid. See Versions and licence ."}, {"t": "Do I need CVSS?", "p": "Troubleshooting and FAQ", "u": "faq.html#do-i-need-cvss", "x": "No. See Reviewing threats ."}, {"t": "Versions and licence", "p": "Versions and licence", "u": "changelog.html", "x": "Versions and licence #"}, {"t": "2.0", "p": "Versions and licence", "u": "changelog.html#20", "x": "Each threat appears once, where it belongs. There are 8 threat families, each with one anchor (see How threats are generated ). The gateway example went from 230 to 138 threats; what disappeared were repeats and threats that could not apply. Fewer properties. The 31 Microsoft core properties that drove no OT threat were removed. A component now has 16 properties instead of 25, a flow 7 instead of 21, and a data store 3 instead of 12. Three flow switches were merged into one: Threats on this Flow . Record-only properties are labelled. Property applicability. Stencils fix the properties that cannot apply (no debug ports, USB, radio or physical exposure on software) or that they imply. The hardware questions gained Not applicable . New properties. Assessment Scope (In scope, Supplier evidence, Out of scope), Runs on a Host Shown in the Model , and Show Data Store Threats for Source Store . Non-network media. Hardwired signal and Local operator input . Channel threats skip them, and wiring threats cover them. Overlaps removed. Generic sniffing, replay and DoS threats step aside where a specific threat covers the same attack. New threats. OT-E23 isolation bypass, OT-T30 and PL-T11 wiring tampering, and data-at-rest threats for read-only stores (IDs ending in R). New stencils: Product Supplier and Development Environment, and Software Supply and Build Pipeline. Upgrading from 1.x # Export your 1.x threat list or report first. The review decisions of repeats that 2.0 drops are not c"}, {"t": "Earlier versions", "p": "Versions and licence", "u": "changelog.html#earlier-versions", "x": "1.3: the plant and site template, and the edge gateway and gas pipeline examples. 1.2: 10 threats added so every ATT&CK for ICS tactic is covered. 1.1: first public version."}, {"t": "Licence", "p": "Versions and licence", "u": "changelog.html#licence", "x": "MIT. You may use, change and share the templates, including commercially. See LICENSE ."}, {"t": "Credits and notes", "p": "Versions and licence", "u": "changelog.html#credits-and-notes", "x": "Stencil icons: Tabler Icons , MIT licence. Microsoft's built-in stencils inside the templates come from TMT's own default template and are Microsoft content. MITRE ATT&CK® is a registered trademark of The MITRE Corporation. IEC 62443 and IEC 61511 are standards of the International Electrotechnical Commission. Clause references are indicative, so check them against the editions you work to. These templates support, but do not replace, the judgement of a qualified OT security and safety team."}];
